{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Usability Testing and Learning Evidence","title":"Module 5 Usability Plan Studio","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting module 5 usability plan studio to moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence","ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"08","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/05/07"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_calibration"]},"content":"# Module 5 Usability Plan Studio\n\n![Module 5 Usability Plan Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-08-module-5-usability-plan-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe pieces of a usability plan can each be excellent and the plan can still be worthless, because the failure mode of a whole plan is not a weak part but parts that do not line up. A precise hypothesis tested with the wrong participants, scored by a mismatched metric, on a task that leaks the answer, decided by a rule that ignores the data: every component is plausible alone, yet the chain is broken. This capstone studio is about internal coherence, the property that makes a plan trace end to end so the metric can actually test the hypothesis, the sample can represent the population, the task can expose the behavior, and the decision rule can follow the evidence. Coherence is what a reviewer checks last and what a designer should design first.\n\nIn AIL 606 the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace is assembled here into one plan, and the studio's job is to catch the seams where chapter 05.01 through 05.07 fail to connect, before a single session burns the budget on a misaligned design.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Audit a full usability plan for alignment across hypothesis, sample, task, metric, observation, and decision rule.\n- Trace each component back to the single learning goal the plan exists to serve.\n- Locate the seam where two well-formed parts contradict each other.\n- Revise the plan so every part puts the same hypothesis at risk.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA draft plan reads, in one breath: hypothesis, the navigation will feel more modern; participants, five expert designers; tasks, click Settings then Profile then Save; metric, satisfaction rating; rule, ship if satisfaction is high. Each clause sounds like something a usability plan should contain, and a quick reader nods along.\n\nA coherence reader does not. The hypothesis is untestable, so the satisfaction metric could never confirm it; the experts cannot represent the real audience; the task spells out the click path, so findability is never tested; and the decision rule lets an attitude number drive a launch the data cannot justify. Nothing is missing, yet nothing connects. The plan would run smoothly and teach nothing, which is the most expensive kind of failure because it looks like success.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nCoherence is a chain, and a chain is only as strong as its weakest link. Auditing means checking each adjacent pair of components for alignment, not each component in isolation.\n\n| Link | Alignment question | Section |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Hypothesis to metric | Can this metric disconfirm this prediction? | 05.01, 05.04 |\n| Population to sample | Do these participants represent the target? | 05.02 |\n| Hypothesis to task | Does the task expose the predicted behavior? | 05.03 |\n| Behavior to observation | Will the protocol capture it verifiably? | 05.05 |\n| Evidence to decision | Does the rule follow what the data can show? | 05.06, 05.07 |\n\nMatch each adjacent link in the chain to the single alignment question that tests whether the two components actually connect.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Hypothesis to metric\",\"definition\":\"Can this metric actually disconfirm this prediction, or does it measure a different construct?\"},{\"term\":\"Population to sample\",\"definition\":\"Do these participants represent the target audience the hypothesis is about?\"},{\"term\":\"Hypothesis to task\",\"definition\":\"Does the task expose the predicted behavior, or does it leak the route and hide it?\"},{\"term\":\"Behavior to observation\",\"definition\":\"Will the protocol capture the behavior verifiably, without judging in real time?\"},{\"term\":\"Evidence to decision\",\"definition\":\"Does the rule follow only what the data, including disaggregated results, can show?\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThe deep idea is that each component constrains its neighbors. A findability hypothesis demands a goal-based task and a completion metric, and it forbids a satisfaction-only metric and a click-path task; choosing one component silently rules out options for the others. This is why coherence cannot be added at the end by polishing parts. **Mayer multimedia learning** supplies the fixed point that holds the chain together: the learning goal, to which every link must trace, so a part that serves no goal is a part that breaks alignment no matter how well-formed it is.\n\nThe same alignment logic recruits the rest of the module's theory. **Cognitive load theory** governs the hypothesis-to-task and behavior-to-observation links, because a task that overloads or a protocol that judges in real time corrupts the very behavior the hypothesis predicts. **UDL learner variability** governs the population-to-sample and evidence-to-decision links, because a sample that misses a subgroup and a decision rule that ignores disaggregated results will both ship a design that fails learners the plan never represented. Coherence, in other words, is where every earlier section's lesson becomes a single integrated check.\n\n## Worked Example: Auditing a Full Usability Plan for Internal Coherence\n\nThis studio assembles the whole chapter into one plan. The failure mode here is not a single weak part; it is a plan whose parts do not line up. Audit a draft plan section by section.\n\n**Before (parts that do not align):**\n\n> Hypothesis: The redesigned navigation will feel more modern. Participants: 5 expert designers. Tasks: \"Click Settings, then Profile, then Save.\" Metric: satisfaction rating. Plan: if satisfaction is high, ship it.\n\nWalk the coherence audit, step by step:\n\n1. **Hypothesis to metric.** \"Feel more modern\" is untestable and satisfaction cannot confirm a navigation-findability claim. Mismatch (chapter 05.01, 05.04).\n2. **Participants to population.** Expert designers do not represent the real audience. Mismatch (05.02).\n3. **Tasks to hypothesis.** The tasks spell out the click path, so findability is never tested. Mismatch (05.03).\n4. **Decision rule to evidence.** \"Ship if satisfaction is high\" lets an attitude metric drive a launch the data cannot justify (05.06).\n\n**After (an aligned plan):**\n\n> Hypothesis: With the redesigned navigation, at least 8 of 10 target users will reach Profile settings unaided, versus a 4 of 10 baseline. Participants: 8 real instructors screened by LMS experience, spanning novice to frequent. Tasks: goal-based (\"Update your display name so students see it\"). Metrics: task completion and time on task (usability) plus a brief findability error count; satisfaction logged but not decisive. Decision rule: revise the navigation if fewer than 8 of 10 succeed, then re-test.\n\nThe after version is defensible because every part traces to the same goal: the metric can test the hypothesis, the sample matches the population, the tasks expose findability, and the decision rule follows the evidence. The studio's job is to catch the before-version mismatches before a single session runs.\n\nAudit the misaligned draft plan link by link below. At each seam, decide whether the two components connect, and watch how a chain of individually plausible parts still fails.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Coherence audit of a plausible-looking plan\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Hypothesis: the navigation will feel more modern. Metric: satisfaction rating. Does the hypothesis-to-metric link hold?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, satisfaction captures feeling modern\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Feel more modern is untestable and satisfaction cannot disconfirm a navigation-findability claim. The first seam is already broken (05.01, 05.04).\"},{\"label\":\"No, the hypothesis is untestable and the metric cannot disconfirm it\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Before going further you must rewrite the hypothesis into a falsifiable findability claim with a completion metric.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You accepted the first link. Participants are 5 expert designers for a course aimed at real instructors. Acceptable?\",\"feedback\":\"No. You also let an untestable hypothesis through. Two flaws now compound: experts cannot represent the audience AND the metric cannot test the claim. Restart the audit at the seams.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Rewritten hypothesis: 8 of 10 target users reach Profile settings unaided, versus a 4 of 10 baseline. Now check population-to-sample: participants are 5 expert designers. Hold?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, designers give expert feedback\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Experts absorb load that real instructors will not, so they cannot represent the target. The population-to-sample link breaks (05.02).\"},{\"label\":\"No, swap to real instructors screened by LMS experience\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the sample must represent the population the hypothesis is about.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"The expert session runs clean and you ship. What happened?\",\"feedback\":\"You shipped on a sample that hid the real friction. A clean expert run is not transferable evidence. The seam between population and sample was the defect.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Last seam: tasks say click Settings then Profile then Save, and the rule is ship if satisfaction is high. What two fixes align the chain?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep the task; it is precise. Keep satisfaction as the gate\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"The spelled-out path tests direction-following not findability (05.03), and satisfaction cannot justify shipping a findability claim (05.06). Both still break.\"},{\"label\":\"Make the task goal-based and let completion, not satisfaction, drive the decision\",\"next\":\"n7\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the task now exposes findability and the decision rule follows the evidence the hypothesis is about.\"}]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"You ran the plan unchanged at these two seams. Result?\",\"feedback\":\"A smooth session that answers no question worth asking: the task hid findability and an attitude rating drove the launch. Fix both seams before running.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n7\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-walk the whole chain. Is the plan coherent now?\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Falsifiable findability hypothesis, representative instructor sample, goal-based task, completion-and-time metrics with satisfaction logged but not decisive, and a revise-if-fewer-than-8-of-10 rule. Every link aligns and every part traces to the same learning goal.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\n<inline-check question=\"A plan has a sharp transfer-learning hypothesis, a representative sample, goal-based tasks, careful behavioral observation, but its only outcome metric is time on task. Where is the broken seam?\" explanation=\"Time on task is a usability metric; it cannot disconfirm a transfer-learning hypothesis. Four links are fine, but the hypothesis-to-metric seam snaps, so the plans strongest claim has no instrument that could ever test it. The defect lives between components, which is exactly what a part-by-part review misses.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The sample is too small for transfer claims\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The hypothesis-to-metric link: time on task cannot test a learning hypothesis\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The observation protocol is judging in real time\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"There is no broken seam; every part is competent\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nAudit the links, not the parts; a plan ships only when every adjacent pair aligns and every component traces to one learning goal. If you can describe each component in isolation but cannot draw a line from each to the same goal, the plan is a collection, not a plan.\n\nBefore finalizing the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace, check three things:\n\n1. **Alignment check:** Does each adjacent pair (hypothesis-metric, population-sample, hypothesis-task, evidence-decision) actually connect?\n2. **Goal-trace check:** Does every component point back to the same learning goal?\n3. **Seam check:** Is there any well-formed part that quietly contradicts its neighbor?\n\nIf any check fails, the plan will run cleanly and answer no question worth asking.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is auditing components in isolation, approving each because it is individually competent and missing that two competent parts contradict each other. A second error is treating coherence as a finishing pass, when the constraints between components mean alignment has to be designed in from the hypothesis outward. A third is assuming a complete plan is a coherent one; completeness only means every slot is filled, not that the slots agree.\n\nA plan with no missing parts can still have no working chain.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one seam from a plausible draft to a fix. A team's plan predicts that an interactive simulation raises transfer accuracy, recruits a representative novice-to-expert sample, writes goal-based tasks, and logs careful behavioral observations: four strong links. Then the metric is time on task, and the seam snaps. Time is a usability measure (05.04); it cannot disconfirm a transfer-learning hypothesis (05.01), so the plan's strongest claim has no instrument that could ever test it. Every neighbor is fine, but this one link is broken, and the whole plan fails through it.\n\nThe fix is local but the diagnosis was global. Swap in a pre/post transfer measure, and the hypothesis-to-metric link aligns; nothing else needs to move. Then re-walk the chain with **UDL learner variability**, checking that the decision rule reads the disaggregated transfer results rather than the mean, so the plan will not ship a simulation that lifts experts and strands novices. The walkthrough's lesson is that coherence auditing finds the one seam that quality-checking each part would miss, because the defect lives between the components, not inside any of them.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a complete plan, keep one column for each component and one for the neighbor it must agree with, then test each pair rather than each box. The defects you are hunting do not live inside the boxes, which usually look fine; they live on the lines between them, which is exactly where a part-by-part review never looks.\n\nA strong reader of a full plan can answer four questions:\n\n1. Can the metric actually disconfirm the hypothesis?\n2. Does the sample represent the population the hypothesis is about?\n3. Does the task expose the behavior the hypothesis predicts?\n4. Does the decision rule follow only what the evidence can support?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe plan is strong when a reviewer can start at any component, follow the chain to the learning goal and back, and find no link where two parts pull in different directions. Treat the seams as the load-bearing elements; a flawless hypothesis wired to a mismatched metric is a broken plan, not a strong plan with one weak spot. Use the annotation tools to mark every adjacency you cannot defend, and ask BigAL to compare your plan against the alignment check rather than to improve any single section.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I changed the metric from time on task to a pre/post transfer measure because the original could not disconfirm the learning hypothesis it was paired with; I rejected the suggestion to keep satisfaction as the decision metric, since an attitude rating cannot justify shipping a transfer claim.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same coherence audit to a grant proposal or a research design, where the components are a research question, a sample, an instrument, and an analysis plan. The failure mode is identical: a sharp question undone by an instrument that cannot measure it, or an analysis that cannot answer it, each part defensible alone. The structure transfers (check the links, trace every part to the question), but the cost of a broken seam rises, because a misaligned study wastes a funded year rather than a test session. A reader who reviews such a proposal section by section, approving each, has repeated the exact mistake the studio exists to prevent.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-08-module-5-usability-plan-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the moderated usability test script with observation-to-revision trace for AIL 606 05.08. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm every adjacent pair in the plan aligns, every component traces to one learning goal, and no well-formed part contradicts its neighbor.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio","stem":"A usability plan states a findability hypothesis but its only metric is a satisfaction rating. As a coherence reviewer, what is the core problem?","options":["The plan needs more participants.","The metric cannot test the hypothesis: a satisfaction rating does not measure whether users can find the feature.","Satisfaction ratings are never allowed in a plan.","The hypothesis should have been removed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A coherent plan requires the metric to be able to confirm or disconfirm the stated hypothesis; satisfaction cannot test findability.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_hypothesis_metric_mismatch","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio","stem":"A plan's hypothesis targets first-time users, but the recruitment line says \"5 expert designers,\" and the tasks list exact click steps. Which two parts are misaligned with the hypothesis?","options":["The metric and the decision rule.","The participant sample (experts, not first-time users) and the tasks (leading click paths that hide findability).","Only the title and the references.","Nothing is misaligned; the plan is fine."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Coherence means every part serves the hypothesis; an expert sample and leading tasks both undercut a first-time-user findability claim.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_parts_dont_align","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to generate a complete usability plan from a one-line brief. What is the defensible boundary on using the output?","options":["Submit the generated plan as your studio artifact since it is complete.","Run the coherence audit yourself, checking that the AI's hypothesis, sample, tasks, metrics, and decision rule all trace to the same goal, and fix the mismatches.","Reject the AI plan entirely; plans must be hand-built end to end.","Use the AI plan only to make the wording sound more professional."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A generated plan can look complete yet be internally incoherent; the designer must audit and reconcile its parts.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_generated_plan_unaudited","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_module_5_usability_plan_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio","stem":"A plan's decision rule reads \"ship if participants seem to like it.\" Even with a good hypothesis, sample, and tasks, why does this break the plan's coherence?","options":["Decision rules are optional in a usability plan.","The decision rule does not connect to the plan's own metrics or thresholds, so the evidence the study gathers cannot actually drive the decision.","Liking a product is the strongest possible evidence.","The rule should have come before the hypothesis."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A coherent plan ties the decision rule to the metrics and thresholds it collects; a vague rule disconnects evidence from the decision.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_8_decision_rule_disconnected","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_5_usability_plan_studio"]}]}}